A||N||5||TRL46202||Design1 Trubo kit questions.....||||1||
Z||000000||TRL46202||05-01-2001||10:42 AM||||Who has this and how hard was it to put on?? also any additional mods that were/needed to be done? Was it worth the $$$$$.<P>Thanks, Tom||||reg||1
Z||000001||grinthock||05-01-2001||10:52 AM||||I am looking into using this kit, the FieroShop in toronto tells me it's a GREAT kit to use, they had one on their Red 85GT (it was running LOW 14's !)<P>It comes with EVERYTHING you need apparantly, and they told me 10 hours to install it (that's $550 Canadian)||||reg||1
Z||000002||turbo86se||05-01-2001||12:23 PM||||I've got the older MillerWoods system on my SE (same owner of Design One). All I've done is port and polish the manifolds, had them coated inside and out with Jet Hot's ceramic coating. I also removed the cat. convertor, stock muffler. The 2.8 itself is completely stock. I've had many conversations with Jim Sutter, pres of IRM (bought and installed) and he said stock is the best, most reliable way to go. I've had zero problems with the turbo system, but I'm on my 2nd auto tranny  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Was it worth the money? The look on that Corvette owner's face when I blew by him (he had a head start, I wasn't paying attention) was worth every penny.<P>I'll be at Williamsburg this summer!  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236791"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||05-01-2001||06:52 PM||||Wonder how the 2.8 turbo compares to a quad4 swap(non-turbo) as far a cost/weight/performance.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Predator||05-02-2001||04:21 PM||||I have the Design One Systems Stage II Turbo setup. It took me two full weekends to install it myself, and I had to have the Oil intake welded on the oil pan for me. I burned up a head gasket first thing out so be sure to check your injectors to make sure they are in excellent condition before you even crank it. I spent $2,700 on the turbo system off of ebay from someone. I would suggest never buying a kit like this used. I spent $1,400 getting the head gaskets fixed, and the injectors cleaned and flow tested. As soon as the shop got it working the turbo itself blew it's seals. Another $1,000 to get that fixed.I had the car Dyno'd and it is running so lean that I cannot even put the car under boost without worring about frying my engine. I have tried to get Kevin at Design One to reply for a fix to the computer control problem with the air/fuel mix, but I can't even get him to respond to emails anymore so now I have to spend more money to come up with an alternative to get the fuel richer under boost. So now I have $5,100 invested in the turbo setup, and I cannot even use it. Is it worth the money? My case is the extreme in bad luck on this, but I wanted you to know what can happen. You can end up spending a crap load of money on this, and once you start you can't just back out. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||saleenfiero||05-02-2001||07:32 PM||||Ouch<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
